$DIR/main.p:7:3: cannot keep &T{...} in p after this iteration: the loop at line 6 makes it in the same memory in every iteration
    A composite literal whose address is taken (at main.p:7) is made in main's
    frame, as a local variable would be.
    Every iteration of the loop at line 6 reuses that memory. When the next
    iteration starts, p would see that iteration's value, not this one's.
    Go gives each iteration of a loop its own variables, so a function literal
    made in one iteration keeps that iteration's values. Plutonium keeps
    variables in the function's frame, which has room for one copy of each;
    giving every iteration its own copy would need memory for each iteration,
    allocated behind the program's back.
    Use &T{...} before the iteration ends, or keep a copy of the value instead
    of a view of it.
    (see "Loop iterations reuse their storage" in the Plutonium spec, section
    18.4)
    (run "pluto explain E3006" for a longer explanation)
